==German==
{{slim-wikipedia|lang=de}}
[[Image:Breads.jpg|thumb|Zwei '''Brote''' — Two '''loaves of bread''' (2)]]
[[Image:Rye bread.JPG|thumb|Mehrere Scheiben Roggen'''brot''' — Several slices of rye '''bread''' (1)]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{m|gmh|brōt}}, from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|brōt}} (attested since the 8th century), from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*braudą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrew-||to seethe, to boil}}; an extension of {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰerə-||to well up, to boil, to be in motion (as of fire or water)}}. Originating from the same root are {{m|de|Brühe}}, {{m|de|Brei}}, {{m|de|braten}} and {{m|de|brennen}}.

''Germanic Cognates'' with identical meaning include Old Saxon {{m|osx|brōd}} (whence Low German {{m|nds|Broot}}, {{m|nds|Brot}}), Old Dutch {{m|odt|*brōd}} (whence Dutch {{m|nl|brood}}, Afrikaans {{m|af|brood}}), Old English {{m|ang|brēad}} (whence Modern English {{m|en|bread}}, Scots {{m|sco|breid}}), West Frisian {{m|fy|brea}} and Old Norse {{m|non|brauð}} (whence Icelandic {{m|is|brauð}}, Faroese {{m|fo|breyð}}, Norwegian {{m|no|brød}}, Swedish {{m|sv|bröd}}, Danish {{m|da|brød}}).

''Non-Germanic-Cognates'' include Ancient Greek {{m|grc|sc=polytonic|βρύτος||fermented barley beverage}}, Latin {{m|la|ferveo||to be hot, to boil, to burn}}, {{m|la|fermentum||fermentation, leaven}}, Albanian {{m|sq|mbruaj||to knead}}, Old Armenian {{m|xcl|բորբ|sc=Armn||bright, aflame}}. Originally, the meaning of ''Brot'' was ''"what has been fermented, leaven"'' and may be a nominal derivative from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*brewwaną||to brew}} (whence German {{m|de|brauen}}). It replaced the older {{m|de|Laib||loaf}} which was the more common term in Old High German (compare the use of {{m|ang|hlāf}} and {{m|ang|brēad}} in Old English).<ref>Pfeifer, Wolfgang. 1995, 2005. ''Etymologisches Wörterbuch des Deutschen.'' München: dtv. ISBN 3423325119.</ref>

Alternative etymologies derive ''Brot'' and its cognates from {{etyl|gem-pro|-}} {{m|gem-pro|*braudaz}}, {{m|gem-pro|*brauþaz||broken piece, fragment}} and from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*bʰera-||to split, beat, hew, struggle}} (compare Swedish {{m|sv|bryta||to break}}, Albanian {{m|sq|brydh||weak, mild}}, English {{m|en|brittle}}). A conflation of both roots could also be possible.

